If you *know* a piece of armour is enchanted to above +3, DON'T
ENCHANT IT ANYMORE. +4 is as far as non-elven armour can go (elven can
be safely enchanted up to +6).  Uncursed ?oEA give one point of
enchantment and blessed scrolls can give either +1 or +2.  If you know
for sure that a piece of armour is at +3 you can read a blessed scroll
and if you're lucky it'll give you 2 points of enchantment and take it
up to +5.  Cursed scrolls take away one point of enchantment so if it
only goes up to +4 you can read one of those to bring it back down to
+3 so you can try again.  This curses your armour, though, so you
probably shouldn't try this unless you have another b?oEA handy to
re-bless it with.  Enchanting armour when you *know* it's above +3,
however, is just asking for trouble.

You have to use the same tactics to get elven armour to +7 - get it to
+5, then read a blessed ?oEA and hope to get lucky.  Always remove all
armour except the piece you want to enchant before reading the scroll
so you don't screw up the rest of your kit.

